ne of the great figures of Romanticism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-bligh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-bligh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William Bligh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Bligh was a Royal Navy officer remembered for the mutiny on HMS Bounty in 1789, after which he sailed some 6,700 kilometres to Timor in an open boat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-bolts"><span class="enc-name">William Bolts</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Bolts was a Dutch-born merchant and former servant of the British East India Company, expelled from Bengal after quarrels with its government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-bradford-governor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-bradford-governor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William Bradford (governor)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Bradford was a Pilgrim leader who sailed on the Mayflower in 1620 and was governor of Plymouth Colony for most years from 1621 until 1657.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-brown-sea-captain"><span class="enc-name">William Brown (sea captain)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Brown was a British sea captain and fur trader on the north-west coast of America in the early 1790s, commanding the ship Butterworth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-c-gorgas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-c-gorgas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William C. Gorgas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William C.</span></a></